      "title": "f) Identification of Fe(III) radical (Fe³⁺)",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "b07b1f6c-f5a4-509d-ae86-73be5bffb0d4",
          "pdf_from": 347,
          "pdf_to": 347,
          "note": "f) Identification of Fe(III) radical (Fe³⁺): a main subheading of 16.7 Tests for identification of basic radicals, its own page (owner 2026-11-04); PDF 347-347."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Take 2 g FeCl₃ in a clean test tube. Dissolve it in 10 cm³ of distilled water. Make two portions of the solution for further tests."
          },
          {
            "type": "table",
            "caption": "Identification of iron (III) radical",
            "header": [
              "Experiment",
              "Observation",
              "Inference"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "To one portion, add NaOH solution.",
                "Orange brown precipitate is formed.",
                "Fe³⁺ ions are indicated."
              ],
              [
                "To the second portion of the above solution, add aqueous ammonia.",
                "Orange brown precipitate is formed.",
                "Fe³⁺ ions are indicated."
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{Fe^{3+}_{(aq)} + 3OH^-_{(aq)} -> $\\underset{\\text{Orange brown ppt}}{\\ce{Fe(OH)3_{(s)}}}$}",
            "text_alt": "Fe³⁺(aq) + 3OH⁻(aq) → Fe(OH)₃(s) (Orange brown ppt)"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
